<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">New Hampshire</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">New <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hampshire">Hampshire</a> is a state in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-england">New England</a>, settled by the English from the 1620s along the Piscataqua river. Its towns were governed by Massachusetts from 1641 until it became a separate royal province in 1679. It disputed the lands west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/connecticut-river">Connecticut River</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>, which became Vermont, and it was one of the thirteen colonies that formed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/new-hampshire.jpg" alt="New Hampshire" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Xrmap flag collection · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_New_Hampshire.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>New <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hampshire">Hampshire</a> is part of the six-state <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-england">New England</a> region. It is bounded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quebec">Quebec</a> to the north and north-west, Maine and the Gulf of Maine to the east, Massachusetts to the south and Vermont to the west. Its main regions include the Great North Woods, the White Mountains, the Lakes Region, the Seacoast and the Merrimack Valley. It has the shortest ocean coastline of any coastal state in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, 18 miles (29 km).</p><p>The White Mountains span the north-central part of the state and include Mount Washington, at 6,288 feet (1,917 m) the tallest peak in the north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rashid-al-din-sinan">Old Man of the Mountain</a>, a face-like rock profile in Franconia Notch, disintegrated in May 2003 but remains a symbol of the state.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Algonquian-speaking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abenaki">Abenaki</a> peoples, largely divided between the Androscoggin, Cowasuck and Pennacook nations, inhabited the area before European colonisation. English and French explorers visited in 1600–1605, and David Thompson settled at Odiorne's Point in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rye">Rye</a> in 1623. The first permanent European settlement was at Hilton's Point, present-day Dover. After several years of petitions from Robert Tufton Mason, the colony became a Royal Province in 1679, the first in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-england">New England</a>. Dover and Durham were burned in 1689 and 1692 during King William's War.</p><p>New <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hampshire">Hampshire</a> was one of the Thirteen Colonies that rebelled against British rule. In 1774 its people seized Fort William and Mary from the British, and in 1776 it became the first of the colonies to establish an independent government and state constitution. In 1788 it was the ninth state to ratify the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitution-of-the-united-states">United States Constitution</a>, bringing it into effect. It was an active centre of abolitionism and fielded close to 32,000 Union soldiers in the Civil War. Rapid industrialisation followed, and the Amoskeag Manufacturing Company in Manchester was the largest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> textile plant in the world.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Concord is the state capital and Manchester the most populous city, and the state has 1,377,529 residents. Its motto, Live Free or Die, reflects its role in the Revolutionary War, and its nickname, the Granite State, refers to its granite formations and quarries. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-canadians">French Canadians</a> formed the most significant influx of immigrants, and a quarter of residents have French American ancestry. The state constitution of 1783 is its supreme law.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “New Hampshire” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Free State Project, founded in 2001, recruited libertarians to move to <b>New Hampshire</b>, where about 6,232 participants had moved by May 2022.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/libertarianism">Libertarianism · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>A Baltimore printer issued it as a broadside, &quot;Defence of Fort M'Henry&quot;, on 17 September; it was in 17 newspapers from Georgia to <b>New Hampshire</b> within weeks, and Thomas Carr of Baltimore published words and music together as &quot;The Star Spangled Banner&quot;.</blockquote><a href="/songs/the-star-spangled-banner">The Star-Spangled Banner</a></li><li><blockquote><b>New Hampshire</b> became the ninth state to ratify, the number required for the Constitution to take effect.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/june-21">21 June in history</a></li><li><blockquote><b>New Hampshire</b>'s ratification a month later made nine, the number needed for the Constitution to take effect.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/may-23">23 May in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Massachusetts absorbed <b>New Hampshire</b> for a time and the Province of Maine.</blockquote><a href="/state/massachusetts-bay-colony">Massachusetts Bay Colony</a></li><li><blockquote>Massachusetts Bay Colony governed the towns from 1641 until the Crown made <b>New Hampshire</b> a royal province in 1679.</blockquote><a href="/state/province-of-new-hampshire">Province of New Hampshire</a></li></ul></section>
